<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Candi Singhasari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1300</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Candi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> temple at Singosari near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malang">Malang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, built around 1300 as a memorial to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a>, the last king of Singhasari, who was killed in 1292. Dedicated mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, it is known for its unfinished carvings, which show how Javanese temple ornament was worked from the top downwards, and for the giant guardian statues that stand nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/candi-singhasari.jpg" alt="Candi Singhasari" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ESCapade · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Candi_Singosari_B.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
